[sliver] The long strip at each tip is the four-bar cover’s flat bearing against its receiver pocket. The production crest is 0.90 mm wide over the entire spring span.
alternatives: Rounding off this bearing restores the narrow contact of the pointed trial. Widening it changes the fitted lead-in and retaining geometry.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/rear-equipment-caps/regulator-snap-cap.step
click: x=16.146 y=20.603 z=18.000
click: x=16.146 y=-20.603 z=18.000

[step] The central regulator bearing ends 1 mm inside the spring ends alongside the outlet collet. This step provides the axial clearance for the collet while the two complete springs reach their receiver end faces.
alternatives: Carrying the bearing to the spring end crowds the release collet. Cutting the springs back shortens both continuous catches.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/rear-equipment-caps/regulator-snap-cap.step
click: x=-12.205 y=-0.600 z=35.000

[thin] The lead-in narrows toward the 0.90 mm flat crest, which is raised 0.40 mm. This continuous taper spreads the leaf over the ledge during assembly.
alternatives: Thickening the ramp would shorten the usable lead-in or widen the four-bar bearing. Both change entry and catch contact at the actual receiver.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/rear-equipment-caps/regulator-snap-cap.step
